              'Consider the function $$f(x)=b^{x}$$ where $$b\\in\\mathbb{R}$$ and $$b>1$$. The graph of $$f$$ contains the point $$\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},4\\right)$$.\n Consider the arithmetic sequence $$\\log_{8}27,\\ \\log_{8}q,\\ \\log_{8}r,\\ \\log_{8}125$$, where $$q>1$$ and $$r>1$$.\n (a) Show that $$b=8$$. [2]\n (b) Write down an expression for $$f^{-1}(x)$$. [1]\n (c) Find the value of $$f^{-1}(\\sqrt{32})$$. [3]\n (d)(i) Show that 27, $$q$$, $$r$$ and 125 are four consecutive terms in a geometric sequence. [4]\n (d)(ii) Find the value of $$q$$ and the value of $$r$$. [5]',
            answer:
              '(a) Shown (b) $$f^{-1}(x)=\\log_8 x$$ (c) $$\\dfrac{5}{6}$$ (d)(ii) $$q=45,\\ r=75$$',
            solution:
              '(a) $$b^{2/3}=4 \\Rightarrow b=4^{3/2}=8$$.\n (b) $$f^{-1}(x)=\\log_8 x$$.\n (c) $$\\log_8\\sqrt{32}=\\tfrac12\\log_8 32=\\tfrac{5}{6}$$.\n (d)(i) Equating differences in the arithmetic sequence leads to $$\\dfrac{q}{27}=\\dfrac{125}{r}=\\dfrac{r}{q}$$, showing 27, q, r, 125 form a geometric sequence.\n (d)(ii) Using $$u_4=u_1r^3$$: $$125=27r^3 \\Rightarrow r_{\\text{ratio}}=\\dfrac53$$; $$q=27\\times\\dfrac53=45$$, $$r=45\\times\\dfrac53=75$$.',
