            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 15,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'All answers in this question should be given to four significant figures. In a local weekly lottery, tickets cost $$\\$2$$ each. In the first week of the lottery, a player will receive $$\\$d$$ for each ticket, with the probability distribution shown in the table below (the probability of receiving $$\\$10$$ is 0.03). The grand prize in the first week of the lottery is $$\\$1000$$.\n $$d$$: 0, 2, 10, 50, Grand Prize; $$P(D=d)$$: 0.85, $$c$$, 0.03, 0.002, 0.0001.\n If nobody wins the grand prize in the first week, the probabilities remain the same, but the value of the grand prize will be $$\\$2000$$ in the second week, and continues to double each week until it is won. All other prize amounts remain the same.\n (a) Find the value of $$c$$. [2]\n (b) Determine whether this lottery is a fair game in the first week. Justify your answer. [4]\n (c) Given that the grand prize is not won and the grand prize continues to double, write an expression in terms of $$n$$ for the value of the grand prize in the $$n$$th week of the lottery. [2]\n (d) The $$w$$th week is the first week in which the player is expected to make a profit. Ryan knows that if he buys a lottery ticket in the $$w$$th week, his expected profit is $$\\$p$$. Find the value of $$p$$. [7]',
            answer:
              '(a) $$c=0.1179$$ (b) Not a fair game, $$E(D)=\\$0.7358$$ (c) $$1000\\times2^{n-1}$$ (d) $$p=\\$0.2358$$',
            solution:
              '(a) $$0.85+c+0.03+0.002+0.0001=1 \\Rightarrow c=0.1179$$.\n (b) $$E(D)=0(0.85)+2(0.1179)+10(0.03)+50(0.002)+1000(0.0001)=0.7358$$; since a fair game requires $$E(D)=\\$2$$ (the ticket price), and $$0.7358\\ne2$$, it is not a fair game.\n (c) Grand prize follows a geometric sequence with $$u_1=1000, r=2$$: $$u_n=1000\\times2^{n-1}$$.\n (d) Require $$E(D)>2$$: $$0.6358+1000\\times2^{n-1}\\times0.0001>2 \\Rightarrow n>4.77$$, so $$w=5$$; expected profit $$=E(D)-2=2.2358-2=0.2358$$.',
            questionType: 'Probability distributions, geometric sequences',
