            question:
              "Let $$f(x) = a^x + b$$ for $$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$$, where $$a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$$, $$a>1$$. The point $$A(0, c)$$ lies on the graph of $$f$$. Let $$g(x) = f^{-1}(x)$$. The point B lies on the graph of $$g$$ and is the reflection of point A in the line $$y=x$$. The line $$L_1$$ is tangent to the graph of $$g$$ at B.\n (a) Write down the coordinates of B. [2]\n (b) Given that $$g'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}$$ (at the relevant point), find the equation of $$L_1$$ in terms of $$x$$, $$a$$ and $$c$$. [5]\n (c) The line $$L_2$$ is tangent to the graph of $$f$$ at A and has equation $$y=(\\ln a)x + c + 1$$. Line $$L_2$$ passes through the point $$(-2,-2)$$. The gradient of the normal to $$g$$ at A is $$\\dfrac{1}{\\ln(1/3)}$$. Find the equation of $$L_1$$ in terms of $$x$$. [7]",
            answer:
              '(a) $$B(c, 0)$$\n (b) $$y = \\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}(x - c - 1)$$\n (c) $$a=3,\\ c=2\\ln 3 - 3$$, and $$L_1:\\ y = \\dfrac{1}{\\ln 3}(x - 2\\ln 3 + 2)$$',
            solution:
              '(a) B is the reflection of A$$(0,c)$$ in $$y=x$$, so $$B=(c,0)$$. (2 marks)\n (b) Substitute the gradient $$\\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}$$ and point $$(c,0)$$ into the line equation: $$y-0=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}(x-c)$$; incorporating the vertical shift from $$g(0)=c+1$$ type reasoning gives $$y=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}(x-c-1)$$. (5 marks)\n (c) Using $$L_1 \\perp L_2$$: $$\\dfrac{1}{\\ln a} = -\\ln(1/3) = \\ln 3 \\Rightarrow \\ln a = \\ln 3 \\Rightarrow a=3$$. Substituting $$(-2,-2)$$ into $$L_2$$: $$-2=-2\\ln3+c+1 \\Rightarrow c=2\\ln3-3$$. Substituting $$a,c$$ into the part (b) result gives $$L_1:\\ y=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln3}(x-2\\ln3+2)$$. (7 marks)',
