            question:
              "Consider the function $$f$$, with derivative $$f'(x) = 2x^2 + 5kx + 3k^2 + 2$$ where $$x, k \\in \\mathbb{R}$$.\n (a) Show that the discriminant of $$f'(x)$$ is $$k^2 - 16$$. [2]\n (b) Given that $$f$$ is an increasing function, find all possible values of $$k$$. [4]",
            answer: '(a) Shown (discriminant $$=k^2-16$$)\n (b) $$-4 \\leq k \\leq 4$$',
            solution:
              "(a) Discriminant $$=(5k)^2-4(2)(3k^2+2)=25k^2-24k^2-16=k^2-16$$. (2 marks)\n (b) For $$f$$ increasing, need $$f'(x)\\geq0$$ for all $$x$$, i.e. discriminant $$\\leq 0$$: $$k^2-16\\leq0 \\Rightarrow -4\\leq k\\leq4$$. (4 marks)",
