            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 15,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              "The diagram shows part of the graph of a quadratic function $$f$$, with vertex at $$(3,4)$$, passing through point Q. The function can be written in the form $$f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k$$. The line $$L$$ is tangent to the graph of $$f$$ at Q. Now consider another function $$y=g(x)$$. The derivative of $$g$$ is given by $$g'(x)=f(x)-d$$, where $$d\\in\\mathbb{R}$$.\n (a) Write down the equation of the axis of symmetry. [1]\n (b)(i) Write down the values of $$h$$ and $$k$$. [2]\n (b)(ii) Point Q has coordinates $$(5,12)$$. Find the value of $$a$$. [2]\n (c) Find the equation of $$L$$. [4]\n (d) Find the values of $$d$$ for which $$g$$ is an increasing function. [3]\n (e) Find the values of $$x$$ for which the graph of $$g$$ is concave-up. [3]",
            answer:
              '(a) $$x=3$$\n (b)(i) $$h=3,\\ k=4$$ (b)(ii) $$a=2$$\n (c) $$y=8x-28$$\n (d) $$d<4$$\n (e) $$x>3$$',
            solution:
              "(a) Axis of symmetry is $$x=3$$ (through the vertex). (1 mark)\n (b)(i) From vertex form, $$h=3,\\ k=4$$. (2 marks)\n (b)(ii) Substitute Q$$(5,12)$$: $$12=a(5-3)^2+4 \\Rightarrow 4a=8 \\Rightarrow a=2$$. (2 marks)\n (c) $$f(x)=2(x-3)^2+4$$, so $$f'(x)=4(x-3)$$; at $$x=5$$, gradient $$=8$$. Equation: $$y-12=8(x-5) \\Rightarrow y=8x-28$$. (4 marks)\n (d) $$g$$ increasing requires $$g'(x)=f(x)-d\\geq0$$ for all $$x$$, i.e. the vertex of $$f(x)-d$$ is at or above the x-axis: $$4-d\\geq0 \\Rightarrow d\\leq4$$ (strict version $$d<4$$ if $$g'(x)>0$$ required). (3 marks)\n (e) $$g$$ concave-up requires $$g''(x)=f'(x)>0$$: $$4(x-3)>0 \\Rightarrow x>3$$. (3 marks)",
