            question:
              'Consider the series $$\\ln x + p\\ln x + \\dfrac13\\ln x + \\ldots$$, where $$x\\in\\mathbb{R},\\ x>1$$ and $$p\\in\\mathbb{R},\\ p\\neq0$$. Consider first the case where the series is geometric.\n (a)(i) Show that $$p=\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}$$. [2]\n (a)(ii) Given that $$p>0$$ and $$S_\\infty=3+\\sqrt3$$, find the value of $$x$$. [3]\n Now consider the case where the series is arithmetic with common difference $$d$$.\n (b)(i) Show that $$p=\\dfrac23$$. [3]\n (b)(ii) Write down $$d$$ in the form $$q\\ln x$$, where $$q\\in\\mathbb{Q}$$. [1]\n (b)(iii) The sum of the first $$n$$ terms of the series is $$-3\\ln x$$. Find the value of $$n$$. [6]',
            answer:
              '(a)(i) Shown\n (a)(ii) $$x=e^2$$\n (b)(i) Shown\n (b)(ii) $$d=-\\dfrac13\\ln x$$\n (b)(iii) $$n=9$$',
            solution:
              '(a)(i) Geometric ratio: $$\\dfrac{p\\ln x}{\\ln x}=\\dfrac{\\frac13\\ln x}{p\\ln x} \\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac{1}{3p} \\Rightarrow p^2=\\dfrac13 \\Rightarrow p=\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}$$. (2 marks)\n (a)(ii) With $$p>0$$, $$r=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}$$. $$S_\\infty=\\dfrac{\\ln x}{1-r}=3+\\sqrt3 \\Rightarrow \\ln x=2 \\Rightarrow x=e^2$$. (3 marks)\n (b)(i) Arithmetic common difference from consecutive terms: $$p\\ln x-\\ln x=\\dfrac13\\ln x-p\\ln x \\Rightarrow 2p\\ln x=\\dfrac43\\ln x \\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac23$$. (3 marks)\n (b)(ii) $$d=p\\ln x-\\ln x=-\\dfrac13\\ln x$$. (1 mark)\n (b)(iii) $$S_n=\\dfrac n2\\left[2\\ln x+(n-1)\\left(-\\dfrac13\\ln x\\right)\\right]=-3\\ln x$$. Dividing by $$\\ln x$$ and simplifying gives a quadratic in $$n$$: $$n^2-7n-18=0 \\Rightarrow (n-9)(n+2)=0 \\Rightarrow n=9$$ (rejecting the negative root). (6 marks)',
