            marks: 16,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'Let $$f(x)=\\dfrac{16}{x}$$. The line $$L$$ is tangent to the graph of $$f$$ at $$x=8$$. $$L$$ can be expressed as $$\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{pmatrix}8\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}+t\\mathbf{u}$$. The direction vector of $$y=x$$ is $$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$$.\n (a) Find the gradient of $$L$$. [2]\n (b) Find $$\\mathbf{u}$$. [2]\n (c) Find the acute angle between $$y=x$$ and $$L$$. [5]\n (d)(i) Find $$(f\\circ f)(x)$$. [3]\n (d)(ii) Hence, write down $$f^{-1}(x)$$. [1]\n (d)(iii) Hence or otherwise, find the obtuse angle formed by the tangent line to $$f$$ at $$x=8$$ and the tangent line to $$f$$ at $$x=2$$. [3]',
            answer:
              "(a) $$f'(8)=-0.25$$\n (b) $$\\mathbf{u}=\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$$ (or scalar multiple)\n (c) $$1.03$$ rad ($$59.0°$$)\n (d)(i) $$(f\\circ f)(x)=x$$\n (d)(ii) $$f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{16}{x}$$\n (d)(iii) $$2.06$$ rad ($$118°$$)",
            solution:
              "(a) $$f'(x)=-16x^{-2}$$, $$f'(8)=-0.25$$ (M1A1)\n (b) $$\\mathbf{u}$$ parallel to $$(1,-0.25)$$, e.g. $$(4,-1)$$ (A2)\n (c) $$\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot(1,1)}{|\\mathbf{u}||(1,1)|}$$; $$\\theta\\approx1.03$$ rad (A1A1A1M1A1)\n (d)(i) $$f(f(x))=\\dfrac{16}{16/x}=x$$ (M1A1A1)\n (d)(ii) Since $$(f\\circ f)(x)=x$$, $$f^{-1}(x)=f(x)=\\dfrac{16}{x}$$ (A1)\n (d)(iii) By symmetry about $$y=x$$, double the angle found in (c): $$2\\times1.03=2.06$$ rad (M1M1A1)",
            questionType: 'Vectors / Tangents / Inverse functions',
            diagram: null,
            subtopicSlug: 'mixed-topics',
