              'The population, $$P$$, of a particular species of marsupial on a small remote island can be modelled by the logistic differential equation $$\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=kP\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{N}\\right)$$ where $$t$$ is time in years and $$k,N$$ are positive constants. The constant $$N$$ represents the maximum sustainable population. Let $$P_0$$ be the initial population.\n \n (a) In the context of the population model, interpret the meaning of $$\\dfrac{dP}{dt}$$. [1]\n (b) Show that $$\\dfrac{d^2P}{dt^2}=k^2P\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{N}\\right)\\left(1-\\dfrac{2P}{N}\\right)$$. [4]\n (c) Hence show that the population increases at its maximum rate when $$P=\\dfrac N2$$. Justify your answer. [5]\n (d) Hence determine the maximum value of $$\\dfrac{dP}{dt}$$ in terms of $$k$$ and $$N$$. [2]\n (e) By solving the logistic differential equation, show that its solution can be expressed as $$kt=\\ln\\dfrac{P}{P_0}\\left(\\dfrac{N-P_0}{N-P}\\right)$$. [7]\n (f) After 10 years, the population of marsupials is $$3P_0$$. It is known that $$N=4P_0$$. Find the value of $$k$$ for this population model. [2]',
            answer:
              '(a) The rate of growth (change) of the marsupial population with respect to time. (d) $$\\dfrac{kN}{4}$$ (f) $$k=\\dfrac1{10}\\ln9\\approx0.220$$',
            solution:
              '(a) Interpretation of the derivative as instantaneous rate of population change.\n (b) Differentiate $$\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP-\\frac{kP^2}{N}$$ implicitly with respect to $$t$$ using the product/chain rule, then substitute back $$\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(1-P/N)$$ and simplify to the given form.\n (c) Set $$\\frac{d^2P}{dt^2}=0\\Rightarrow P=0,\\frac N2,N$$; use a sign diagram or second-derivative test to show $$P=\\frac N2$$ gives a local maximum of $$\\frac{dP}{dt}$$.\n (d) Substitute $$P=\\frac N2$$ into $$\\frac{dP}{dt}$$: maximum value $$=\\frac{kN}{4}$$.\n (e) Separate variables: $$\\int\\frac{N}{P(N-P)}dP=\\int k\\,dt$$; use partial fractions $$\\frac{N}{P(N-P)}=\\frac1P+\\frac1{N-P}$$, integrate to get $$\\ln P-\\ln(N-P)=kt+c$$; apply $$P=P_0$$ at $$t=0$$ to find $$c$$, giving the stated result.\n (f) Substitute $$t=10,\\ P=3P_0,\\ N=4P_0$$: $$10k=\\ln(9)\\Rightarrow k\\approx0.220$$.',
