            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: false,
            question:
              'Consider the functions $$f,g:\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R}$$ defined by $$f((x,y))=(x+y,\\,x-y)$$ and $$g((x,y))=(xy,\\,x+y)$$.\n (a)(i) Find $$(g\\circ f)((x,y))$$.\n (a)(ii) Find $$(f\\circ g)((x,y))$$.\n (b) State, with a reason, whether or not $$f$$ and $$g$$ commute.\n (c) Find the inverse of $$f$$.',
            answer:
              '(a)(i) $$(x^2-y^2,\\,2x)$$\n (a)(ii) $$((x+y)(x-y),\\,2x)=(x^2-y^2,\\,2x)$$\n (b) No, $$f\\circ g \\ne g\\circ f$$ in general\n (c) $$f^{-1}((x,y))=\\left(\\dfrac{x+y}{2},\\,\\dfrac{x-y}{2}\\right)$$',
            solution:
              '(a)(i) $$(g\\circ f)((x,y))=g((x+y,x-y))$$ (M1) $$=((x+y)(x-y),\\,(x+y)+(x-y))=(x^2-y^2,\\,2x)$$ A1A1.\n (a)(ii) $$(f\\circ g)((x,y))=f((xy,x+y))=(xy+x+y,\\,xy-x-y)$$ A1A1.\n (b) No, since $$f\\circ g \\ne g\\circ f$$ R1 (accept counter-example).\n (c) Set $$f((x,y))=(a,b) \\Rightarrow x+y=a,\\,x-y=b$$ (M1); solve $$x=\\frac{a+b}{2},\\,y=\\frac{a-b}{2}$$ (M1); $$f^{-1}((x,y))=\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2},\\frac{x-y}{2}\\right)$$ A1.',
