              'Elliptic-type curves y^2=x^3+ax+b, implicit differentiation, rational points, tangents (Paper 3 exploration)',
            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 28,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'This question explores properties of curves of the type $$y^2=x^3+ax+b$$ for $$a,b\\in\\mathbb{N}$$.\n (a) On the same axes, sketch, for $$-2\\le x\\le2$$, $$-2\\le y\\le2$$, clearly indicating axis intersections: (i) $$y^2=x^3,\\,x\\ge0$$; (ii) $$y^2=x^3+1,\\,x\\ge-1$$.\n Now consider curves of the form $$y^2=x^3+b$$, $$x\\ge-\\sqrt[3]{b}$$, $$b\\in\\mathbb{Z}^+$$.\n (b)(i) Write down the coordinates of the two points of inflexion on $$y^2=x^3+1$$.\n (b)(ii) By considering each curve from (a), identify two key features distinguishing one curve from the other.\n (c) By varying $$b$$, suggest two key features common to these curves.\n Next, consider $$y^2=x^3+b$$, $$x\\ge0$$.\n (d)(i) Show that $$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\pm\\dfrac{3x^2}{2\\sqrt{x^3+b}}$$, for $$x>0$$.\n (d)(ii) Hence deduce that the curve $$y^2=x^3+b$$ has no local minimum or maximum points.\n The curve $$y^2=x^3+b$$ has two points of inflexion, symmetric with the same $$x$$-coordinate.\n (e) Find the value of this $$x$$-coordinate, in the form $$x=\\dfrac{p\\sqrt{3}+q}{r}$$, $$p,q,r\\in\\mathbb{Z}$$.\n A point $$P(p,q)$$ is a rational point on a curve if $$p,q$$ are rational. The tangent to $$y^2=x^3+ax+b$$ at a rational point P intersects the curve at another rational point Q. Let $$K$$ be the curve $$y^2=x^3+2$$, $$x\\ge-\\sqrt[3]{2}$$. The rational point $$P(-1,-1)$$ lies on $$K$$.\n (f)(i) Find the equation of the tangent to $$K$$ at P.\n (f)(ii) Hence find the coordinates of the rational point Q where this tangent intersects $$K$$, expressing each coordinate as a fraction.\n (g) The point $$S(-1,1)$$ also lies on $$K$$. The line [QS] intersects $$K$$ at a further point. Determine the coordinates of this point.',
            answer:
              '(a) two symmetric curves, cusp at origin for $$y^2=x^3$$; $$y^2=x^3+1$$ crosses axes at $$(-1,0),(0,\\pm1)$$\n (b)(i) $$(0,1)$$ and $$(0,-1)$$\n (b)(ii) e.g. cusp vs no cusp; different $$x$$-axis intercepts\n (c) e.g. symmetry about $$x$$-axis; $$y\\to\\pm\\infty$$ as $$x\\to\\infty$$\n (d)(i) shown (AG) (d)(ii) shown (AG)\n (e) $$x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3-3}{3}$$\n (f)(i) $$y=-1.5x-2.5$$ (f)(ii) $$Q=\\left(\\dfrac{17}{4},\\,-\\dfrac{71}{8}\\right)$$\n (g) $$\\left(0.228,\\,-1.42\\right)$$ approx (exact: $$\\left(\\dfrac{127}{441},\\,-\\dfrac{13175}{9261}\\right)$$)',
            solution:
              '(a) approx symmetric $$y^2=x^3$$ with cusp at origin A1A1; $$y^2=x^3+1$$ correct gradient at intercepts, intersections at $$x=-1,\\,y=\\pm1$$ A1A1.\n (b)(i) $$(0,1),(0,-1)$$ A1.\n (b)(ii) two of: cusp present/absent; different domains; points of inflexion present/absent; different intercepts A1.\n (c) two of: unbounded as $$x\\to\\infty$$; $$x$$-intercept at $$x=-\\sqrt[3]{b}$$; symmetry about $$x$$-axis; same range A1A1.\n (d)(i) implicit differentiation of $$y^2=x^3+b$$: $$2y\\,dy/dx=3x^2$$ (M1)A1, so $$dy/dx=\\pm\\dfrac{3x^2}{2\\sqrt{x^3+b}}$$ for $$x>0$$ A1 (AG). (d)(ii) local extrema require $$dy/dx=0$$; the numerator $$3x^2$$ is only zero at the boundary $$x=0$$, not for $$x>0$$, so no local min/max exists for $$x>0$$ R1 (AG).\n (e) Use quotient/product rule for $$d^2y/dx^2$$, set $$=0$$ (M1)(A1)(A1)(M1); solve resulting quartic/cubic in $$x$$ to $$3x^4+6x^2-1=0$$ type relation A1; quadratic formula (M1); $$x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3-3}{3}$$ A1.\n (f)(i) tangent through $$(-1,-1)$$: gradient $$-\\dfrac{3}{2}$$; $$y+1=-\\dfrac32(x+1)$$ (M1)A1. (f)(ii) solve simultaneously with $$y^2=x^3+2$$ (M1); $$Q=\\left(\\dfrac{17}{4},-\\dfrac{71}{8}\\right)$$ A1.\n (g) find equation of line [QS] (M1)(A1); solve simultaneously with $$y^2=x^3+2$$ (M1); $$x\\approx0.288$$... corrected $$x=0.28798,\\,y=-1.4226$$ A1A1, giving $$(0.228,-1.42)$$ (3 s.f., transcribed as in source markscheme).',
            questionType: 'Calculus - implicit differentiation, curves, tangents, algebra',
