            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 27,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'This question investigates conditions for the existence of complex roots of polynomial equations of degree 3 and 4. The cubic $$x^3+px^2+qx+r=0$$, $$p,q,r\\in\\mathbb{R}$$, has roots $$\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$$.\n (a) By expanding $$(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta)(x-\\gamma)$$, show that $$p=-(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)$$, $$q=\\alpha\\beta+\\beta\\gamma+\\gamma\\alpha$$, $$r=-\\alpha\\beta\\gamma$$.\n (b)(i) Show that $$p^2-2q=\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2$$.\n (b)(ii) Hence show that $$(\\alpha-\\beta)^2+(\\beta-\\gamma)^2+(\\gamma-\\alpha)^2=2p^2-6q$$.\n (c) Given that $$p^2<3q$$, deduce that $$\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$$ cannot all be real.\n Consider $$x^3-7x^2+kx+1=0$$, $$k\\in\\mathbb{R}$$.\n (d) Using the result from (c), show that when $$k=17$$, this equation has at least one complex root.\n Noah believes that if $$p^2\\ge3q$$ then $$\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$$ are all real.\n (e)(i) By varying $$k$$ in $$x^3-7x^2+kx+1=0$$, determine the smallest positive integer value of $$k$$ required to show that Noah is incorrect.\n (e)(ii) Explain why the equation will have at least one real root for all values of $$k$$.\n Now consider quartic equations $$x^4+px^3+qx^2+rx+s=0$$, $$p,q,r,s\\in\\mathbb{R}$$, with roots $$\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,\\delta$$, where $$p=-(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma+\\delta)$$, $$q=\\sum \\alpha\\beta$$ (all pairs), $$r=-\\sum\\alpha\\beta\\gamma$$ (all triples), $$s=\\alpha\\beta\\gamma\\delta$$.\n (f)(i) Find an expression for $$\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2$$ in terms of $$p$$ and $$q$$.\n (f)(ii) Hence state a condition in terms of $$p$$ and $$q$$ that would imply $$x^4+px^3+qx^2+rx+s=0$$ has at least one complex root.\n (g) Use your result from (f)(ii) to show that $$x^4-2x^3+3x^2-4x+5=0$$ has at least one complex root.\n The equation $$x^4-9x^3+24x^2+22x-12=0$$ has one integer root.\n (h)(i) State what the result in (f)(ii) tells us when considering this equation.\n (h)(ii) Write down the integer root of this equation.\n (h)(iii) By writing $$x^4-9x^3+24x^2+22x-12$$ as a product of one linear and one cubic factor, prove that the equation has at least one complex root.',
            answer:
              '(a) shown (AG)\n (b)(i)/(ii) shown (AG)\n (c) shown (AG)\n (d) $$49<51$$, so at least one complex root\n (e)(i) $$k=12$$ (e)(ii) a cubic always crosses the $$x$$-axis at least once / complex roots occur in conjugate pairs so the third root must be real\n (f)(i) $$\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2=p^2-2q$$\n (f)(ii) $$p^2<2q$$\n (g) $$4<6$$, so at least one complex root\n (h)(i) $$81>2(24)$$, so nothing can be deduced\n (h)(ii) $$-1$$\n (h)(iii) $$(x+1)(x^3-10x^2+34x-12)=0$$; for the cubic $$100<102$$ so it has at least one complex root, hence the quartic does too',
            solution:
              '(a) Expand $$(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta)(x-\\gamma)$$ M1A1, compare coefficients with $$x^3+px^2+qx+r$$ A1 (AG for all three).\n (b)(i) $$p^2-2q=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)^2-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\beta\\gamma+\\gamma\\alpha)=\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2$$ (A1)(M1)A1 (AG). (b)(ii) expand $$(\\alpha-\\beta)^2+(\\beta-\\gamma)^2+(\\gamma-\\alpha)^2=2(\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2)-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\beta\\gamma+\\gamma\\alpha)=2(p^2-2q)-2q=2p^2-6q$$ M1A1A1 (AG).\n (c) $$p^2<3q \\Rightarrow 2p^2-6q<0 \\Rightarrow$$ sum of squares $$<0$$ A1; if all roots real, sum of squares of real differences $$\\ge0$$ R1; contradiction, so not all real (AG).\n (d) $$p^2=49,\\,3q=51$$ A1; $$49<51\\Rightarrow p^2<3q\\Rightarrow$$ at least one complex root R1.\n (e)(i) GDC investigation, $$k=12$$ (M1)A1. (e)(ii) complex roots occur in conjugate pairs, so a cubic (odd degree, real coefficients) always has at least one real root R1.\n (f)(i) Expand $$(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma+\\delta)^2$$ (M1)(A1), rearrange: $$\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2=p^2-2q$$ A1. (f)(ii) $$p^2<2q$$ (equivalently $$p^2-2q<0$$) A1.\n (g) $$p=-2,q=3\\Rightarrow p^2=4,2q=6$$; $$4<6$$ R1, hence at least one complex root (AG).\n (h)(i) $$p=-9,q=24\\Rightarrow p^2=81,2q=48$$; $$81>48$$ so condition not met, nothing can be deduced R1.\n (h)(ii) $$x=-1$$ A1 (by inspection/GDC).\n (h)(iii) Factorise: $$(x+1)(x^3-10x^2+34x-12)$$ M1A1A1; for the cubic factor $$p=-10,q=34\\Rightarrow p^2=100,3q=102$$; $$100<102$$ R1, so the cubic (and hence the quartic) has at least one complex root (AG).',
            questionType:
              'Complex numbers - polynomial roots, symmetric functions, algebraic proof',
