            answer: 'Proof — see mark scheme',
            solution:
              '(a) $$E(X)=(k+1)\\int_0^1 x^{k+1}dx=(k+1)\\left[\\dfrac{x^{k+2}}{k+2}\\right]_0^1=\\dfrac{k+1}{k+2}$$ (M1)(A1, AG).\n (b) $$\\text{Var}(X)=E(X^2)-[E(X)]^2=(k+1)\\int_0^1x^{k+2}dx-\\left(\\dfrac{k+1}{k+2}\\right)^2=\\dfrac{k+1}{k+3}-\\left(\\dfrac{k+1}{k+2}\\right)^2$$ (M1)(A1). Combine over a common denominator and simplify the numerator to $$(k+1)$$ (M1)(A1) $$\\Rightarrow\\text{Var}(X)=\\dfrac{k+1}{(k+2)^2(k+3)}$$ (AG).',
            questionType: 'Statistics — Continuous Random Variables / Proof / Algebra',
            diagram: null,
            subtopicSlug: 'continuous-random-variables',
