            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'The population, $$P$$, of a particular species of marsupial on a small remote island can be modelled by the logistic differential equation $$\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=kP\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{N}\\right)$$ where $$t$$ is time in years and $$k,N$$ are positive constants; $$N$$ is the maximum sustainable population. Let $$P_0$$ be the initial population.\n a. In the context of the population model, interpret the meaning of $$\\dfrac{dP}{dt}$$. [1]\n b. Show that $$\\dfrac{d^2P}{dt^2}=k^2P\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{N}\\right)\\left(1-\\dfrac{2P}{N}\\right)$$. [4]\n c. Hence show that the population increases at its maximum rate when $$P=\\dfrac{N}{2}$$. Justify your answer. [5]\n d. Hence determine the maximum value of $$\\dfrac{dP}{dt}$$ in terms of $$k$$ and $$N$$. [2]\n e. By solving the logistic differential equation, show that its solution can be expressed as $$kt=\\ln\\dfrac{P}{P_0}\\left(\\dfrac{N-P_0}{N-P}\\right)$$. [7]\n f. After 10 years, the population is $$3P_0$$. It is known that $$N=4P_0$$. Find the value of $$k$$ for this population model. [2]',
            answer:
              'a. The rate of change (growth rate) of the marsupial population with respect to time. c. Maximum growth rate occurs at $$P=N/2$$ (justified via sign change of $$d^2P/dt^2$$). d. $$\\left(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\right)_{\\max}=\\dfrac{kN}{4}$$. f. $$k=0.220$$',
            solution:
              'b. Implicit differentiation (product rule) of the logistic ODE (AG). c. Set $$d^2P/dt^2=0\\Rightarrow P=0,\\,N/2,\\,N$$; sign diagram or second-derivative test shows $$P=N/2$$ gives a local maximum of $$dP/dt$$. d. Substitute $$P=N/2$$ into the logistic ODE. e. Separate variables, use partial fractions $$\\frac{N}{P(N-P)}=\\frac1P+\\frac1{N-P}$$, integrate, apply initial condition, rearrange (AG). f. Substitute $$t=10,P=3P_0,N=4P_0$$ into the solution: $$10k=\\ln9\\Rightarrow k=0.220$$.',
            questionType: 'Differential equations (logistic model)',
