            calculator: true,
            question:
              'This question investigates regular $$n$$-sided polygons inscribed/circumscribed in a circle of radius 1, and uses the perimeter as $$n\\to\\infty$$ to approximate $$\\pi$$. Let $$P_i(n)$$ be the perimeter of an inscribed $$n$$-gon and $$P_c(n)$$ the perimeter of a circumscribed $$n$$-gon (circle radius 1).\n (a) An equilateral triangle ABC of side $$x$$ is inscribed in the circle; O is the centre, dividing ABC into 3 isosceles triangles each subtending $$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$$ at O. Using right-angled trigonometry (or otherwise) show the perimeter of ABC is $$3\\sqrt{3}$$.\n (b) A square of side $$x$$ is inscribed in the circle; divide it into 4 isosceles triangles and find the exact perimeter of the square.\n (c) Find the perimeter of a regular hexagon of side $$x$$ inscribed in the circle.\n (d) Show that $$P_i(n)=2n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{n}\\right)$$.\n (e) Use an appropriate Maclaurin series expansion to find $$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}P_i(n)$$ and interpret the result geometrically.\n (f) Show that $$P_c(n)=2n\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{n}\\right)$$.\n (g) By writing $$P_c(n)$$ in the form $$\\dfrac{2\\tan(\\pi/n)}{1/n}$$, find $$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}P_c(n)$$.\n (h) Use the results from (d) and (f) to determine an inequality for $$\\pi$$ in terms of $$n$$.\n (i) Determine the least value of $$n$$ such that the lower and upper bound approximations from (h) are both within 0.005 of $$\\pi$$.',
            answer:
              '(a) $$3\\sqrt{3}$$ (b) $$4\\sqrt{2}$$ (c) $$6$$ (d) shown (e) $$2\\pi$$; polygon tends to the circle of radius 1 (f) shown (g) $$2\\pi$$ (h) $$n\\sin(\\pi/n)<\\pi<n\\tan(\\pi/n)$$ (i) $$n=46$$',
            solution:
              "(a) In right triangle OCX, $$\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3}=\\frac{x/2}{1}\\Rightarrow x=\\sqrt{3}$$, perimeter $$=3\\sqrt3$$ AG.\n (b) $$\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{1}{x}\\Rightarrow x=\\sqrt2$$, perimeter $$=4\\sqrt2$$.\n (c) 6 equilateral triangles $$\\Rightarrow x=1$$, perimeter $$=6$$.\n (d) $$\\sin(\\pi/n)=\\frac{x/2}{1}\\Rightarrow x=2\\sin(\\pi/n)$$; $$P_i=n\\cdot x=2n\\sin(\\pi/n)$$ AG.\n (e) Using $$\\sin\\theta=\\theta-\\theta^3/6+\\dots$$: $$2n\\sin(\\pi/n)=2\\pi-\\pi^3/(3n^2)+\\dots\\to 2\\pi$$; as $$n\\to\\infty$$ the polygon becomes the circle of radius 1, $$P_i\\to 2\\pi$$.\n (f) In each of $$2n$$ right triangles with angle $$\\pi/n$$ at centre, $$x/2=\\tan(\\pi/n)$$, $$P_c=2n\\tan(\\pi/n)$$ AG.\n (g) Indeterminate form $$0/0$$; L'Hopital gives $$\\lim=2\\pi$$.\n (h) $$P_i<2\\pi<P_c\\Rightarrow n\\sin(\\pi/n)<\\pi<n\\tan(\\pi/n)$$.\n (i) Numerically searching bounds gives $$n=46$$.",
