            question:
              'Boris recorded the number of daylight hours on the first day of each month in a northern hemisphere town. Data was plotted and joined by a smooth curve with minimum point (0, 8) and maximum point (6, 16).\n \n Let the curve be $$y=f(t)$$, $$t$$ = time in months. Boris thinks $$f(t)$$ might be modelled by a quadratic function. Paula thinks a better model is $$f(t)=a\\cos(bt)+d$$, $$t\\ge0$$.\n \n The true maximum number of daylight hours was 16 hours 14 minutes.\n \n a. Write down one reason why a quadratic function would not be a good model for the number of hours of daylight per day, across a number of years. [1]\n b.i. State the amplitude. [1]\n b.ii. State the period. [1]\n b.iii. State the equation of the principal axis. [2]\n c. Hence or otherwise find the equation of this model in the form $$f(t)=a\\cos(bt)+d$$. [3]\n d. For the first year of the model, find the length of time when there are more than 10 hours and 30 minutes of daylight per day. [4]\n e. Calculate the percentage error in the maximum number of daylight hours Boris recorded in the diagram. [3]',
