            subtopicDetail: 'Voronoi diagrams - equations of bisectors, coordinate geometry',
            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 18,
            marksBreakdown: '2+4+3+2+4+2+1',
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'A Voronoi diagram shows four supermarkets with coordinates $$A(0,0)$$, $$B(6,0)$$, $$C(0,6)$$ and $$D(2,2)$$, with vertices X, Y, Z. All distances are in km. The equation of (XY) is $$y=2-x$$ and the equation of (YZ) is $$y=0.5x+3.5$$. The coordinates of Y are $$(-1,3)$$ and Z are $$(7,7)$$. A town planner believes the larger the area of Voronoi cell XYZ, the more people shop at supermarket D.\n a. Find the midpoint of [BD].\n b. Find the equation of (XZ).\n c. Find the coordinates of X.\n d. Determine the exact length of [YZ].\n e. Given that the exact length of [XY] is $$\\sqrt{32}$$, find the size of $$X\\hat{Y}Z$$ in degrees.\n f. Hence find the area of triangle XYZ.\n g. State one criticism of this interpretation.',
            answer:
              "a. $$(4,1)$$\n b. $$y=2x-7$$\n c. $$(3,-1)$$\n d. $$4\\sqrt5$$\n e. $$\\approx71.6°$$\n f. $$24$$ km$$^2$$\n g. A larger Voronoi cell area doesn't necessarily mean more shoppers (e.g. transport links, other stores, uneven population density)",
            solution:
              'a. Midpoint of B(6,0) and D(2,2): $$\\left(\\frac{2+6}{2},\\frac{2+0}{2}\\right)=(4,1)$$.\n b. Gradient of BD is $$-\\frac12$$, so perpendicular bisector (XZ) has gradient $$2$$: $$y-1=2(x-4)\\Rightarrow y=2x-7$$.\n c. Solve $$y=2-x$$ and $$y=2x-7$$ simultaneously: $$2-x=2x-7\\Rightarrow x=3,\\ y=-1$$, so $$X(3,-1)$$.\n d. $$YZ=\\sqrt{(7-(-1))^2+(7-3)^2}=\\sqrt{80}=4\\sqrt5$$.\n e. Cosine rule: $$XZ=\\sqrt{80}$$; $$\\cos(X\\hat{Y}Z)=\\frac{80+32-80}{2\\sqrt{80}\\sqrt{32}}\\approx0.316\\Rightarrow X\\hat{Y}Z\\approx71.6°$$.\n f. Area $$=\\frac12\\sqrt{80}\\sqrt{32}\\sin(71.6°)=24$$ km$$^2$$.\n g. E.g., factors other than proximity (transport, specialization, non-uniform population density) affect shopping choice, so area alone is not a reliable measure.',
