            subtopic: 'Complex numbers',
            subtopicDetail: 'Sinusoidal modelling, angular speed, complex exponential form',
            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 25,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'A suitable site for the landing of a spacecraft on the planet Mars is identified at a point, $$A$$. The shortest time from sunrise to sunset at point $$A$$ must be found. Radians should be used throughout this question. All values given in the question should be treated as exact.\n \n Mars completes a full orbit of the Sun in $$669$$ Martian days, which is one Martian year. On day $$t$$, where $$t\\in\\mathbb{Z}$$, the length of time, in hours, from the start of the Martian day until sunrise at point $$A$$ can be modelled by a function $$R(t)$$, where $$R(t)=a\\sin(bt)+c,\\ t\\in\\mathbb{R}$$. The graph of $$R$$ (shown for one Martian year) is periodic with period $$669$$ days.\n \n Mars completes a full rotation on its axis in $$24$$ hours $$40$$ minutes. The time of sunrise on Mars depends on the angle, $$\\delta$$, at which it tilts towards the Sun. During a Martian year, $$\\delta$$ varies from $$-0.440$$ to $$0.440$$ radians. The angle, $$\\theta$$, through which Mars rotates on its axis from the start of a Martian day to the moment of sunrise, at point $$A$$, is given by $$\\cos\\theta=0.839\\tan\\delta,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$$.\n \n Let $$S(t)$$ be the length of time, in hours, from the start of the Martian day until sunset at point $$A$$ on day $$t$$. $$S(t)$$ can be modelled by the function $$S(t)=1.5\\sin(0.00939t+2.83)+18.65$$.\n \n The length of time between sunrise and sunset at point $$A$$, $$D(t)$$, can be modelled by the function $$D(t)=1.5\\sin(0.00939t+2.83)-1.6\\sin(0.00939t)+d$$. Let $$g(t)=1.5\\sin(0.00939t+2.83)-1.6\\sin(0.00939t)$$ and hence $$D(t)=g(t)+d$$. $$g(t)$$ can be written in the form $$\\text{Im}(z_1-z_2)$$, where $$z_1$$ and $$z_2$$ are complex functions of $$t$$.\n \n (a) Show that $$b\\approx0.00939$$.\n (b) Find the angle through which Mars rotates on its axis each hour.\n (c)(i) Show that the maximum value of $$\\theta=1.98$$, correct to three significant figures.\n (c)(ii) Find the minimum value of $$\\theta$$.\n (d)(i) Find the maximum value of $$R(t)$$.\n (d)(ii) Find the minimum value of $$R(t)$$.\n (e) Hence show that $$a=1.6$$, correct to two significant figures.\n (f) Find the value of $$c$$.\n (g) Find the value of $$d$$.\n (h)(i) Write down $$z_1$$ and $$z_2$$ in exponential form, with a constant modulus.\n (h)(ii) Hence or otherwise find an equation for $$D$$ in the form $$D(t)=p\\sin(qt+s)+d,\\ p,q,s,d\\in\\mathbb{R}$$.\n (h)(iii) Find, in hours, the shortest time from sunrise to sunset at point $$A$$ that is predicted by this model.',
            answer:
              '(a) $$b=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{669}\\approx0.00939$$\n (b) $$\\approx0.255$$ radians per hour ($$14.6^{\\circ}$$ per hour)\n (c)(ii) $$\\theta_{\\min}=1.16$$\n (d)(i) $$R_{\\max}\\approx7.76$$ hours\n (d)(ii) $$R_{\\min}\\approx4.57$$ hours\n (f) $$c\\approx6.17$$\n (g) $$d\\approx12.5$$\n (h)(i) $$z_1=1.5e^{(0.00939t+2.83)i},\\ z_2=1.6e^{(0.00939t)i}$$\n (h)(ii) $$D(t)\\approx3.06\\sin(0.00939t+2.99)+12.5$$\n (h)(iii) $$\\approx9.42$$ hours',
            solution:
              '(a) Period $$=669\\Rightarrow b=\\frac{2\\pi}{669}\\approx0.00939190\\ldots\\approx0.00939$$. AG [2 marks, verified]\n (b) Martian day length $$=24\\frac{2}{3}$$ hours; angular speed $$=\\frac{2\\pi}{24\\frac23}\\approx0.2547$$ rad/hr $$(\\approx14.59^{\\circ}/\\text{hr})$$. [3 marks, verified]\n (c)(i) Substitute $$\\delta=-0.440$$: $$\\cos\\theta=0.839\\tan(-0.440)\\Rightarrow\\theta=1.97684\\ldots\\approx1.98$$. AG [3 marks, verified with numeric arccos]\n (c)(ii) Substitute $$\\delta=0.440$$: $$\\theta=1.16474\\ldots\\approx1.16$$. [1 mark, verified]\n (d)(i) $$R_{\\max}=\\frac{\\theta_{\\max}}{\\text{angular speed}}=\\frac{1.97684}{0.25472}=7.76075\\ldots\\approx7.76$$ hours. [2 marks, verified]\n (d)(ii) $$R_{\\min}=\\frac{1.16474}{0.25472}=4.57258\\ldots\\approx4.57$$ hours. [1 mark, verified]\n (e) $$a=\\frac{R_{\\max}-R_{\\min}}{2}=\\frac{7.76075-4.57258}{2}\\approx1.594\\approx1.6$$ (2 sf). AG [2 marks, verified]\n (f) $$c=\\frac{R_{\\max}+R_{\\min}}{2}=\\frac{7.76075+4.57258}{2}=6.1667\\approx6.17$$. [2 marks, verified]\n (g) $$d=18.65-c=18.65-6.1667\\approx12.48$$ ($$\\approx12.5$$). [2 marks, verified]\n (h)(i) Write each sinusoidal term as the imaginary part of a complex exponential: $$z_1=1.5e^{(0.00939t+2.83)i}$$, $$z_2=1.6e^{(0.00939t)i}$$. [3 marks]\n (h)(ii) $$z_1-z_2=e^{0.00939ti}\\left(1.5e^{2.83i}-1.6\\right)$$; compute $$1.5e^{2.83i}-1.6\\approx3.0625e^{2.9909i}$$ (modulus $$\\approx3.06$$, argument $$\\approx2.99$$), so $$D(t)\\approx3.06\\sin(0.00939t+2.99)+12.5$$. [4 marks, verified with complex-number arithmetic in Python: modulus $$=3.0625$$, argument $$=2.9909$$ rad, matching mark scheme]\n (h)(iii) Shortest sunrise-to-sunset time $$=d_{\\max}-\\text{amplitude}=12.4833-3.0625\\approx9.42$$ hours. [2 marks, verified: $$9.4208$$ hours to 4 sf]',
            questionType: 'Trigonometry / Modelling / Complex numbers',
