            solution:
              "a.i. GDC linear regression: $$Q'(t) = 3094.27\\ldots Q(t) - 54042.3\\ldots$$ A1A1\n a.ii. $$r = 0.754741\\ldots$$ A1\n a.iii. $$t$$ is not a random variable, or data is not bivariate normal, or not a random sample, or data appears non-linear, or $$r$$ only measures linear correlation R1\n b.i. Separate variables (M1): $$\\int \\dfrac{1}{Q}\\,dQ = \\int \\beta N\\,dt$$; $$\\ln|Q| = \\beta N t + c$$ A1A1A1 (equivalently $$Q = Ae^{\\beta N t}$$)\n b.ii. Exponential regression (M1): $$Q = 1.14864\\ldots e^{0.292055\\ldots t}$$ A1\n b.iii. $$R^2 = 0.999431\\ldots$$ A1\n b.iv. Compare $$R^2$$ and $$r$$ (M1): $$0.999 > 0.755^2 (=0.563)$$; exponential model is the better fit A1\n b.v. Model predicts more infected computers than the entire population (unlimited growth) R1\n c. $$1.15e^{0.292t}=2.3$$ or $$t=\\ln2/0.292$$ M1; $$t \\approx 2.37$$ days A1\n d. Calculate $$\\beta$$ for city X: $$\\beta = 0.292055\\ldots/(2.6\\times10^6)$$ M1 $$\\approx 1.12328\\times10^{-7}$$ A1; larger than $$9.64\\times10^{-8}$$, so virus spreads more easily in city X R1\n e. $$a=38.3,\\ b=3086.1$$ A1A1\n f.i. $$\\dfrac{Q'}{Q} = 0.422228\\ldots - 2.5561\\times10^{-6}Q$$ (A1)(A1); intercept $$=k$$, gradient $$=-k/L$$ (M1); $$k \\approx 0.422$$ A1; $$L \\approx 165000$$ A1\n f.ii. Recognize $$L$$ is the eventual number infected (M1); $$165205\\ldots/2600000 \\approx 6.35\\%$$ A1",
