            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              "A transformation $$T$$ of a plane is represented by $$\\mathbf r'=M\\mathbf r+\\mathbf p$$, where $$M$$ is a $$2\\times2$$ matrix, $$\\mathbf p$$ a $$2\\times1$$ vector. Triangle $$OAB$$ with coordinates $$(0,0)$$, $$(0,1)$$, $$(1,0)$$ is transformed by $$T$$ to $$(0,1)$$, $$\\left(\\tfrac14,1+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\right)$$, $$\\left(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4},\\tfrac34\\right)$$ respectively. $$M=RS$$ where $$S$$ represents an enlargement scale factor $$0.5$$ centre $$(0,0)$$, and $$R$$ represents a rotation about $$(0,0)$$. $$T$$ can also be described as an enlargement scale factor $$\\tfrac12$$ centre $$(u,v)$$ followed by a rotation about the same centre $$(u,v)$$.\n a.i. By considering the image of $$(0,0)$$, find $$\\mathbf p$$. [2]\n a.ii. By considering the images of $$(1,0)$$ and $$(0,1)$$, show that $$M=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}&\\frac14\\\\-\\frac14&\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\end{pmatrix}$$. [4]\n b. Write down the matrix $$S$$. [1]\n c.i. Use $$M=RS$$ to find the matrix $$R$$. [4]\n c.ii. Hence find the angle and direction of the rotation represented by $$R$$. [3]\n d.i. Write down an equation satisfied by $$\\begin{pmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{pmatrix}$$. [1]\n d.ii. Find the value of $$u$$ and the value of $$v$$. [3]",
