            calculator: false,
            question:
              'The simple, complete graph $$K_n\\ (n>2)$$ has vertices $$A_1, A_2, A_3, \\ldots, A_n$$. The weight of the edge from $$A_i$$ to $$A_j$$ is $$i+j$$.\n (a)(i) Draw the graph $$K_4$$, including the weights of all edges. (ii) Use the nearest-neighbour algorithm, starting at $$A_1$$, to find a Hamiltonian cycle. (iii) Hence find an upper bound for the travelling salesman problem for $$K_4$$. [4]\n (b) Consider $$K_5$$. Use the deleted vertex algorithm, with $$A_5$$ deleted, to find a lower bound for the travelling salesman problem for $$K_5$$. [5]\n (c)(i) Use the nearest-neighbour algorithm, starting at $$A_1$$, to find a Hamiltonian cycle for general $$K_n$$. (ii) Hence find and simplify an expression in $$n$$ for an upper bound for the travelling salesman problem for $$K_n$$. [7]\n (d) By splitting the weight of edge $$A_iA_j$$ into two parts, show that all Hamiltonian cycles of $$K_n$$ have the same total weight, equal to the answer in (c)(ii). [3]',
            answer:
              '(a)(iii) Upper bound $$=20$$ (cycle $$A_1A_2A_3A_4A_1$$: $$3+5+7+5$$). (b) Lower bound $$=25$$. (c)(ii) Upper bound $$=n(n+1)$$. (d) Every Hamiltonian cycle of $$K_n$$ has total weight $$n(n+1)$$ (shown).',
            solution:
              "(a)(i) $$K_4$$ with edge weights $$A_1A_2=3,\\ A_1A_3=4,\\ A_1A_4=5,\\ A_2A_3=5,\\ A_2A_4=6,\\ A_3A_4=7$$ (A1 graph, A1 weights). (ii) Cycle $$A_1A_2A_3A_4A_1$$ (A1). (iii) Upper bound $$=3+5+7+5=20$$ (A1). (b) With $$A_5$$ deleted, Kruskal's MST on $$A_1$$-$$A_4$$ uses $$A_1A_2(3), A_1A_3(4), A_1A_4(5)$$ (M1A1); reconnect $$A_5$$ via its two cheapest edges $$A_5A_1(6), A_5A_2(7)$$ (M1A1); lower bound $$=3+4+5+6+7=25$$ (A1). (c)(i) Cycle $$A_1A_2A_3\\ldots A_{n-1}A_nA_1$$ (A1A1). (ii) Smallest edge from $$A_1$$ is $$A_1A_2=3$$; smallest new edge from $$A_2$$ is $$A_2A_3=5$$; pattern continues to $$A_{n-1}A_n=2n-1$$; final edge $$A_nA_1=n+1$$ (M1). Sum $$=3+5+7+\\cdots+(2n-1)+(n+1) = \\frac{n-1}{2}(2n+2)+(n+1) = n(n+1)$$ (M1A1A1). (d) Assign weight $$i$$ to vertex $$A_i$$'s end of each edge $$A_iA_j$$ (split $$i+j$$ into $$i$$ and $$j$$) (M1). Each vertex $$A_i$$ contributes weight $$i$$ on entry and $$i$$ on exit in any Hamiltonian cycle, since each vertex is visited exactly once (R1). Total weight $$=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} 2i = n(n+1)$$, independent of cycle order (A1, AG).",
