            answer:
              "(a)(i) A circuit is a walk that begins and ends at the same vertex with no repeated edges. (a)(ii) An Eulerian circuit is a circuit containing every edge of the graph. (b) $$G'$$ cannot have an Eulerian circuit. (c) Example graphs given (each with 3 vertices of degree 2 and 2 vertices of odd degree, one graph and its complement).",
            solution:
              "(a)(i) A circuit is a walk that begins and ends at the same vertex and has no repeated edges (A1). (a)(ii) An Eulerian circuit is a circuit that contains every edge of a graph (A1). (b) If $$G$$ has an Eulerian circuit, all 6 vertices have even degree (2 or 4) (A1). In $$K_5$$-complement terms, each vertex has degree $$5-\\deg_G(v)$$ in $$G'$$ (since $$K_6$$ has degree 5 per vertex); hence $$G'$$ must have all vertices of odd degree (1 or 3) (R1). Therefore $$G'$$ cannot have an Eulerian circuit (R1). (c) Example: a 5-vertex graph with exactly 2 odd-degree vertices and 3 vertices of degree 2, together with its complement satisfying the same property (A2 for $$G$$, A2 for $$G'$$).",
