            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 8,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'The strength of earthquakes is measured on the Richter magnitude scale (0 to 8). The Gutenberg–Richter equation gives the average number of earthquakes per year, $$N$$, with magnitude at least $$M$$: $$\\log_{10}N=a-M$$, for some $$a\\in\\mathbb{R}$$, equivalently $$N=\\dfrac{10^a}{10^M}$$. This region has an average of 100 earthquakes per year with magnitude at least 3. The most severe earthquake recorded had magnitude 7.2. The number of earthquakes in a given year with magnitude at least 7.2 can be modelled by a Poisson distribution with mean $$\\lambda$$, independent from year to year. Let $$Y$$ be the number of years between the magnitude-7.2 earthquake and the next earthquake of at least this magnitude.\n a. Find the value of $$a$$. [2]\n b. Find the value of $$N$$ for magnitude at least 3 restated (i.e. find $$10^a$$). [2]\n c. Find the average number of earthquakes in a year with magnitude at least 7.2. [1]\n d. Find $$P(Y>100)$$. [3]',
            answer:
              'a. $$a=5$$; b. $$10^a=100000$$; c. $$\\lambda\\approx0.00631$$; d. $$P(Y>100)\\approx0.532$$',
            solution:
              'a. $$\\log_{10}100=a-3 \\Rightarrow a=5$$ (M1A1). b. $$10^a=10^5=100000$$ (M1A1). c. $$\\lambda=\\dfrac{10^5}{10^{7.2}}=0.00631$$ (A1). d. No earthquakes in the first 100 years: model as Po$$(0.00631\\times100)$$ or Po$$(0.00631)$$ repeated 100 times, giving $$P(Y>100)=P(\\text{count}=0)=0.532$$ (M1M1A1).',
            questionType: 'Poisson distribution / log-linear models',
            diagram: null,
            subtopicSlug: 'poisson-distribution',
