            question:
              "An accountancy firm studies whether interview rating predicts staying with the firm (contingency table: Stay/Leave × Excellent/Very good/Good = 12,20,19 / 10,15,24). A new cohort completes a written assessment; a stratified sample of size 18 is drawn from 91 new employees (55 national, 36 international). Written-assessment marks and manager performance scores (1–10) are recorded for national (11 employees) and international (7 employees) departments; a scatter diagram is given. The international department's 7 employees retake the written assessment (test-retest reliability) with two sets of marks. Five sections of the written assessment are compared against five professional attributes (25 correlation tests total) at the 5% level, assuming no true correlation and independence between tests.\n a. Use an appropriate test (5% level) to determine whether staying with the firm is independent of interview rating, stating hypotheses, $$p$$-value, and conclusion. [6]\n b. Show that 11 employees are selected for the sample from the national department. [2]\n c.i. Without calculation, explain why it might not be appropriate to calculate a correlation coefficient for the whole sample of 18 employees. [2]\n c.ii. Find Spearman's $$r_s$$ for the seven international-department employees. [4]\n c.iii. Comment on the validity of the written assessment as a measure of performance in this department, justifying your answer. [2]\n d.i. State the name of this type of reliability test. [1]\n d.ii. Test $$H_0:\\rho=0$$ vs $$H_1:\\rho>0$$ at 5% for the first/second written-assessment marks (international department). [4]\n d.iii. Hence comment on the reliability of the written assessment. [1]\n e.i. Write down the number of correlation tests carried out. [1]\n e.ii. Assuming no true correlation and independent test outcomes, find the probability that at least one test is significant. [4]\n e.iii. A significant result is obtained comparing Section 2 and attribute X. Interpret this result. [1]",
            answer:
              '$$\\chi^2$$ independence $$p=0.487$$ (independent); national sample $$=11$$; $$r_s(\\text{international})=0.909$$; test-retest $$p=0.00209$$ (reliable); $$P(\\ge1\\text{ significant of }25)=0.723$$',
            solution:
              'a. $$\\chi^2$$ test for independence; $$H_0$$: staying and rating independent; $$H_1$$: not independent; $$p=0.487$$; $$p>0.05$$, insufficient evidence to reject $$H_0$$. M1A1A2R1A1\n b. $$\\dfrac{55}{91}\\times18=10.9\\approx11$$. M1A1\n c.i. Scatter suggests the two departments differ (international manager appears less generous), so a single correlation for the pooled group is misleading. A1R1\n c.ii. Rank both variables (ties averaged); $$r_s=0.909$$. (M1)(A1)(M1)(A1)\n c.iii. Strong association between written mark and manager score ⇒ written assessment is likely a valid measure. A1R1\n d.i. Test-retest (reliability). A1\n d.ii. $$p=0.00209<0.05$$; reject $$H_0$$, i.e. significant positive correlation between first and second marks. A2R1A1\n d.iii. The test appears reliable. A1\n e.i. 25. A1\n e.ii. $$P(\\ge1)=1-0.95^{25}=0.723$$. (M1)(M1)A1A1\n e.iii. Although significant, with 25 tests a significant result is likely to occur by chance, so should be treated cautiously / further evidence sought. R1',
