            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              'Aimmika records bags of rice sold each day over 90 days (frequencies for 0–10 bags: 1,8,12,11,19,14,13,8,2,0,2). She believes this follows a Poisson distribution with historic mean 4.2. Expected frequencies (Poisson, mean 4.2) are given for classes $$\\le1,2,3,4,5,6,7,\\ge8$$: $$a,11.903,16.665,b,14.698,10.289,6.173,c$$. She tests goodness of fit at 5%. She claims advertising (300 THB/day) will increase sales; the owner Nichakarn disputes it will raise profit. Over 60 days of advertising, 282 bags are sold with profit 495 THB/bag.\n a.i. Find the mean and variance for the 90-day sample data. [2]\n a.ii. Hence state why Aimmika believes her data follows a Poisson distribution. [1]\n b. State one assumption needed to support a Poisson model for daily rice sales. [1]\n c. Find the values of $$a$$, $$b$$, and $$c$$, to 3 decimal places. [5]\n d.i. Write down the degrees of freedom for her goodness-of-fit test. [1]\n d.ii. Perform the $$\\chi^2$$ goodness-of-fit test and state, with reason, a conclusion. [7]\n e.i. By finding a critical value, perform a test at 5% to determine if the 60-day sales (282 bags) show an increase over the historic rate. [6]\n e.ii. Hence state the probability of a Type I error for this test. [1]\n f. By considering both claims, explain whether the advertising was beneficial to the store. [3]',
            answer:
              'Mean $$=4.233$$, variance $$=4.268$$; $$a=7.018,\\ b=17.498,\\ c=5.756$$; df$$=7$$; $$\\chi^2$$ $$p=0.728$$ (fits Poisson); critical value $$=279$$ bags, $$282\\ge279$$ reject $$H_0$$ (increase); $$P(\\text{Type I})=0.0493$$; advertising increases sales but is not profitable overall',
            solution:
              'a.i. Mean $$=4.233$$; variance $$=4.268$$. A1A1\n a.ii. Mean $$\\approx$$ variance, consistent with Poisson. A1\n b. Sales on different days are independent, occurring at a constant mean rate. A1\n c. $$a=7.018,\\ b=17.498$$; $$c=90-(\\text{sum of others})=5.756$$ (or via $$P(X\\ge8)$$). (M1)A1A1(M1)A1A1\n d.i. df$$=7$$. A1\n d.ii. $$H_0$$: data follows Poisson(4.2); $$H_1$$: does not; group tails so expected $$\\ge5$$; $$p=0.728>0.05$$; insufficient evidence to reject $$H_0$$ — data follows a Poisson distribution. A1A1M1A2R1A1\n e.i. $$H_0:\\mu=252,\\ H_1:\\mu>252$$ (or per-day $$\\mu=4.2$$); find critical value using normal approx / Poisson sum; critical value $$=279$$; $$282\\ge279$$; reject $$H_0$$ — advertising increased sales. M1A1M1A1R1A1\n e.ii. $$P(\\text{Type I})\\approx0.0493$$. A1\n f. Extra profit from increased sales (\\$14,850) is less than advertising cost (\\$18,000) over 60 days, so despite increasing bags sold, advertising did not increase overall profit. M1A1R1',
