            subtopicDetail: "Coupled linear systems — substitution, Euler's method, stability",
            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 29,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: true,
            question:
              "This question investigates the solution to a coupled system of differential equations and how to transform it to a system that can be solved by the eigenvector method.\n \n It is desired to solve the coupled system of differential equations $$\\dot{x} = x + 2y - 50, \\quad \\dot{y} = 2x + y - 40,$$ where $$x$$ and $$y$$ represent the population of two types of symbiotic coral and $$t$$ is time measured in decades.\n \n (a) Find the equilibrium point for this system.\n (b) If initially $$x=100$$ and $$y=50$$, use Euler's method with a time increment of 0.1 to find an approximation for the values of $$x$$ and $$y$$ when $$t=1$$.\n (c) Extend this method to conjecture the limit of the ratio $$\\dfrac{x}{y}$$ as $$t \\to \\infty$$.\n (d) Show how using the substitution $$u=x-10$$, $$v=y-20$$ transforms the system into $$\\dot{u}=u+2v,\\ \\dot{v}=2u+v$$.\n (e) Solve this system by the eigenvalue method and hence find the general solution for $$\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}$$ of the original system.\n (f) Find the particular solution to the original system, given the initial conditions of part (b).\n (g) Hence find the exact values of $$x$$ and $$y$$ when $$t=1$$, giving the answers to 4 significant figures.\n (h) Use part (f) to find the limit of the ratio $$\\dfrac{x}{y}$$ as $$t \\to \\infty$$.\n (i) With the initial conditions given in part (b), state if the equilibrium point is stable or unstable.\n (j) If instead the initial conditions were $$x=20$$ and $$y=10$$, find the particular solution for $$\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}$$ of the original system.\n (k) With the initial conditions as given in part (j), determine if the equilibrium point is stable or unstable.",
            answer:
              '(a) $$x=10, y=20$$. (b) $$x(1)\\approx 848, y(1)\\approx 837$$. (c) $$\\lim x/y = 1$$. (e) $$\\begin{pmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{pmatrix}=ae^{-t}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}+be^{3t}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$$. (f) $$a=30,\\ b=60$$. (g) $$x(1)=1226, y(1)=1214$$ (4 s.f.). (h) $$\\lim x/y=1$$. (i) Unstable. (j) $$a=10,\\ b=0$$, so $$\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}=10e^{-t}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix}10\\\\20\\end{pmatrix}$$. (k) Stable.',
            solution:
              '(a) Solve $$x+2y-50=0,\\ 2x+y-40=0 \\Rightarrow x=10, y=20$$. (b) Iterate $$x_{n+1}=x_n+0.1(x_n+2y_n-50)$$, $$y_{n+1}=y_n+0.1(2x_n+y_n-40)$$ ten times from $$(100,50)$$. (c) Extend the table of iterations; ratio tends to 1. (d) Since $$\\dot u=\\dot x,\\ \\dot v=\\dot y$$, substitute $$x=u+10,y=v+20$$ into the original equations to get the given transformed system. (e) Eigenvalues from $$\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda&2\\\\2&1-\\lambda\\end{vmatrix}=0 \\Rightarrow \\lambda=-1,3$$ with eigenvectors $$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$$ and $$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$$; add back $$\\begin{pmatrix}10\\\\20\\end{pmatrix}$$. (f) Solve $$100=a+b+10,\\ 50=-a+b+20$$ for $$a,b$$. (g) Substitute $$t=1$$. (h) Dominant term is $$60e^{3t}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$$, giving ratio 1. (i) Growing $$e^{3t}$$ term present, so unstable. (j) Solve $$20=a+b+10,\\ 10=-a+b+20$$ for $$a,b$$. (k) Since the $$e^{3t}$$ coefficient $$b=0$$, only the decaying term remains, so the equilibrium is stable.',
