            difficulty: 'Hard',
            marks: 13,
            marksBreakdown: null,
            calculator: false,
            question:
              'Consider the system of paired differential equations $$\\dot{x}=ax+by, \\quad \\dot{y}=cx+dy.$$ This system is solved using the eigenvalue method.\n \n (a) Show that if the system has two distinct real eigenvalues then $$(a-d)^2+4bc>0.$$\n (b)(i) Find two conditions that must be satisfied by $$a,b,c,d$$ if the system has a pair of purely imaginary eigenvalues.\n (b)(ii) Explain why $$b$$ and $$c$$ must have opposite signs.\n (c) In the case of purely imaginary eigenvalues, the solution forms an ellipse large enough to cross both the positive and negative $$x$$ axes and the positive and negative $$y$$ axes. By considering the differential equations at these four crossing points, investigate whether the trajectory is clockwise or anticlockwise round the ellipse. Give your decision in terms of $$b$$ and $$c$$, and using part (b)(ii) show that your conclusions are consistent.',
            answer:
              '(a) $$(a-d)^2+4bc>0$$ (shown). (b)(i) $$a+d=0$$ and $$b^2+... $$ — specifically $$a=-d$$ and $$(a-d)^2+4bc<0 \\Rightarrow d^2+bc<0$$. (b)(ii) Since $$d^2+bc<0$$, $$bc<0$$, so $$b,c$$ have opposite signs. (c) If $$b>0$$ the trajectory is anticlockwise; if $$b<0$$ it is clockwise (equivalently reversed in terms of $$c$$) — consistent with (b)(ii).',
            solution:
              '(a) Characteristic equation $$\\lambda^2-(a+d)\\lambda+(ad-bc)=0 \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{(a+d)\\pm\\sqrt{(a+d)^2-4(ad-bc)}}{2}$$. For two distinct real roots require $$(a+d)^2-4(ad-bc)>0 \\Rightarrow (a-d)^2+4bc>0$$. (b)(i) For purely imaginary eigenvalues require $$a+d=0$$ and $$(a-d)^2+4bc<0 \\Rightarrow d^2+bc<0$$. (b)(ii) $$d^2+bc<0 \\Rightarrow bc<0$$, so $$b$$ and $$c$$ have opposite signs. (c) On the $$x$$-axis, $$y=0$$ so $$\\dot y=cx$$; sign of $$\\dot y$$ at positive/negative $$x$$-crossings determines rotation direction in terms of $$c$$; on the $$y$$-axis, $$x=0$$ so $$\\dot x=by$$, giving direction in terms of $$b$$; since $$b,c$$ have opposite signs (from (b)(ii)), both approaches agree on the same rotation direction.',
