            question:
              '(a) Find the value of $$\\displaystyle\\int_{4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^3}\\,dx.$$\n (b) Illustrate graphically the inequality $$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=5}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3} < \\int_{4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^3}\\,dx < \\sum_{n=4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}.$$\n (c) Hence write down a lower bound for $$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}.$$\n (d) Find an upper bound for $$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}.$$',
            answer:
              '(a) $$\\dfrac{1}{32}$$. (c) Lower bound $$\\dfrac{1}{32}$$. (d) Upper bound $$\\dfrac{3}{64}$$ (or $$\\dfrac{1}{18}$$, depending on method).',
            solution:
              "(a) $$\\int_{4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^3}dx=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}x^{-2}\\right]_4^R=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}(R^{-2}-4^{-2})\\right)=\\frac{1}{32}$$. (b) Sketch $$y=1/x^3$$ with rectangles of width 1 starting at $$x=4$$: at least three upper rectangles (over-estimate, sum from $$n=4$$) and three lower rectangles (under-estimate, sum from $$n=5$$), showing lower sum < integral < upper sum. (c) A lower bound is the value from part (a): $$\\frac{1}{32}$$. (d) Method 1: $$\\sum_{n=5}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}<\\frac{1}{32} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{64}+\\sum_{n=5}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}<\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{1}{64} \\Rightarrow \\sum_{n=4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}<\\frac{3}{64}$$. Method 2: shift the inequality's lower limit to get $$\\sum_{n=4}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^3}<\\int_3^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^3}dx=\\frac{1}{18}$$.",
            questionType: 'Improper integrals, series, integral test, proof/estimation',
            diagram: null,
            subtopicSlug: 'integration',
