            calculator: true,
            question:
              "This question models the spread of a computer virus predicting the number of computers in a city infected. A systems analyst defines $$t$$ = number of days since the first computer was infected, and $$Q(t)$$ = total number of computers infected up to and including day $$t$$. Data collected for city X (2.6 million computers):\n \n $$t: 10,15,20,25,30,35,40 \\qquad Q(t): 20,90,403,1806,8070,32667,120146$$\n \n A model for the early stage suggests $$Q'(t)=\\beta N Q(t)$$, where $$N$$ is the total number of computers in a city and $$\\beta$$ measures how easily the virus spreads; both assumed constant. For city Y, $$\\beta=9.64\\times10^{-8}$$.\n \n An estimate for $$Q'(t),\\ t\\ge5$$, is $$Q'(t)\\approx\\dfrac{Q(t+5)-Q(t-5)}{10}$$, giving estimates for city X: at $$t=15, Q'=a$$; $$t=20, Q'=171.6$$; $$t=25, Q'=766.7$$; $$t=30, Q'=b$$; $$t=35, Q'=11207.6$$.\n \n An improved model valid for large $$t$$ is the logistic differential equation $$Q'(t)=kQ(t)\\left(1-\\dfrac{Q(t)}{L}\\right)$$, where $$k$$ and $$L$$ are constants. The graph of $$\\dfrac{Q'(t)}{Q(t)}$$ against $$Q(t)$$ is predicted to be a straight line.\n \n (a)(i) Find the equation of the regression line of $$Q(t)$$ on $$t$$.\n (a)(ii) Write down the value of $$r$$, Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient.\n (a)(iii) Explain why it would not be appropriate to conduct a hypothesis test on the value of $$r$$ found in (a)(ii).\n (b)(i) Find the general solution of the differential equation $$Q'(t)=\\beta N Q(t).$$\n (b)(ii) Using the data in the table, write down the equation for an appropriate non-linear (exponential) regression model.\n (b)(iii) Write down the value of $$R^2$$ for this model.\n (b)(iv) Hence comment on the suitability of the model from (b)(ii) in comparison with the linear model from part (a).\n (b)(v) By considering large values of $$t$$, write down one criticism of the model found in (b)(ii).\n (c) Use your answer from part (b)(ii) to estimate the time taken for the number of infected computers to double.\n (d) Find in which city, X or Y, the computer virus is spreading more easily. Justify your answer using your results from part (b).\n (e) Determine the value of $$a$$ and of $$b$$. Give your answers correct to one decimal place.\n (f)(i) Use linear regression to estimate the value of $$k$$ and of $$L$$.\n (f)(ii) The solution to the logistic differential equation is $$Q(t)=\\dfrac{L}{1+Ce^{-kt}}$$ where $$C$$ is a constant. Using your answer to (f)(i), estimate the percentage of computers in city X expected to have been infected over a long period of time.",
            answer:
              '(a)(i) $$Q(t)\\approx3090t-54000$$. (a)(ii) $$r=0.755$$. (b)(ii) $$Q(t)\\approx1.15e^{0.292t}$$. (b)(iii) $$R^2=0.999$$. (c) $$\\approx2.37$$ days. (d) City X spreads the virus more easily. (e) $$a=38.3,\\ b=3086.1$$. (f)(i) $$k\\approx0.422,\\ L\\approx165000$$. (f)(ii) $$\\approx6.35\\%$$.',
            solution:
              "(a)(i)-(ii) Linear regression of $$Q(t)$$ on $$t$$ using GDC: $$Q=3094.27t-54042.3$$, $$r=0.755$$. (a)(iii) $$t$$ is not a random variable / data is not bivariate normal, so a hypothesis test on $$r$$ is inappropriate. (b)(i) $$\\int\\frac{1}{Q}dQ=\\int\\beta N\\,dt \\Rightarrow \\ln Q=\\beta N t+c \\Rightarrow Q=Ae^{\\beta N t}$$. (b)(ii)-(iii) Exponential regression on GDC: $$Q=1.1486e^{0.292055t}$$, $$R^2=0.999431$$. (b)(iv) Compare $$R^2$$ (0.999) to $$r^2$$ (0.570): the exponential model has a much better fit. (b)(v) Exponential growth is unbounded, eventually exceeding the total population of computers — unrealistic. (c) Solve $$1.15e^{0.292t}=2.3 \\Rightarrow t=\\ln2/0.292\\approx2.37$$. (d) City X's rate constant $$\\beta=0.292055/2.6\\times10^6\\approx1.123\\times10^{-7}$$, larger than city Y's $$9.64\\times10^{-8}$$, so X spreads more easily. (e) Interpolate/extrapolate using $$Q'(t)\\approx\\frac{Q(t+5)-Q(t-5)}{10}$$ at $$t=15$$ and $$t=30$$. (f)(i) Linear regression of $$Q'/Q$$ on $$Q$$: $$\\frac{Q'}{Q}=0.42228-2.5561\\times10^{-6}Q$$; intercept $$=k=0.422$$, gradient $$=-k/L \\Rightarrow L=165205$$. (f)(ii) $$L$$ is the eventual number infected: $$165205/2600000\\approx6.35\\%$$.",
